---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'privacy' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Privacy"
  description="No comment, code, or diff content is collected or transmitted by the extension. See exactly what stays in your browser and what can be synced.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g18 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow acc">privacy:</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(29px,3.8vw,41px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.031em;line-height:1.05">The extension doesn't send what you write in a review anywhere.</h1>
          <p class="lede">Review comments can be more sensitive than the code itself: they contain not only technical information, but also what people think about someone else's work. <strong>The extension does not collect, remotely store, or transmit the content of your code, comments, or diffs.</strong> This page explains exactly what data exists, where it lives, and under what conditions anything can leave your browser.</p>
          <p class="small">Last revised: November 12, 2026.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="ct">scope</span>
          <p style="margin:0">This page covers the <strong>browser extension</strong>, which is the whole product. The toolkit once planned a second component that would have run outside the browser — a companion server, then a verifier; both are abandoned, so there is no other part of the product for your data to reach. The extension is not listed in the browser stores yet: today it is installed from a release archive.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">the extension:</span>
          <h2>What lives where</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="scroller">
          <table class="t wide">
            <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Does it leave the browser?</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Your comments, code, and diffs</strong></td>
                <td class="d">In the page, inside the code platform's own interface.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>never</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Interface language, direct shortcuts, and button order</td>
                <td class="d"><code>chrome.storage.sync</code></td>
                <td><span class="st fb"><span class="dot"></span>synced</span><span class="why">Only through your browser's own synchronization mechanism, if you have enabled it. No Reefact service is involved. Only these three preferences are synchronized.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Your login and the logins of comment authors currently displayed</td>
                <td class="d">In memory for the current tab, read from the page.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">Neither stored nor transmitted. They disappear when the tab closes.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Configuration for the repository being displayed</td>
                <td class="d">Retrieved using your existing browser session — your session's cookies, never a token of the extension's own — and kept in memory for the tab.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">The extension reads a resource your session is already allowed to access, just as your browser would if you opened it yourself. It reads it once more before blocking a comment.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Whether that configuration could be read</td>
                <td class="d">On your device, in the extension's local storage: the current state, and the reason when the read failed. It is what the “Status” section of the options page displays.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Configuration notices from the last read</td>
                <td class="d">On your device, in the extension's local storage: the host and path of the repository that was read, and, for each notice, its kind, its code, its text and what it cites — a configuration key or value, a version, the level at fault. When a value was rejected, the notice keeps <strong>that value in full</strong>, as written in the configuration: an allowlist pattern or a tool command.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">Replaced at every read; no history is kept.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Your organization's policy floor, if it deploys one</td>
                <td class="d">In the browser's managed storage, which your organization's browser policy fills. The extension only reads it.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Optional domains you have granted access to</td>
                <td class="d">The list is managed by the browser. The platform you chose for each domain is stored on your device.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Local journal: what the extension did not recognize on a page, and its own errors</td>
                <td class="d">On your device, in the extension's local storage. One line per selector rule, fifty at most, each keeping that rule's most recent observation: its name, when it failed, the selectors it tried, the platform, and <strong>the page's address</strong> at that moment — query parameters and fragment included — along with the last five distinct addresses where the rule failed.<br><br>Each line comes with a record of the interface around the failure: the tag and a closed list of attributes of a few nearby elements, and a compressed skeleton of the page built the same way — one megabyte at most for all skeletons, the oldest erased first. <strong>Never the page's text</strong>: no comment, no code, no field value, not even the text of a button. The values of those attributes are kept, though, so an interface label can name what the page shows — an account, for instance.<br><br>The journal also keeps the errors of the extension's own code — one line per message, with the message and its stack truncated — and a log of how the extension put its script in place in your browser: forty entries at most, with the address patterns of the sites it serves and no page address.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">The journal is written whether or not telemetry is on. It leaves your device only if you share it: “Download the diagnostic (.zip)”, on the options page, gathers it into an archive for a bug report, and “Clear the log” erases all of it in one click. If you allowed telemetry, only the name of a failing rule is sent — never an address, and never an error.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Usage telemetry</td>
                <td class="d">Nothing is emitted by default.</td>
                <td><span class="st fb"><span class="dot"></span>only if you allow it</span><span class="why">Only if you explicitly allow it and all three conditions below are met.</span></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small measure">The extension stores <strong>no authentication token and no secret</strong>. It does not authenticate directly against your code platform's API. Anything it needs to read outside the current page is accessed through URLs your existing browser session is already authorized to open.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">telemetry:</span>
          <h2>Telemetry requires three separate conditions.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Nothing is sent unless <strong>all three</strong> conditions are satisfied. If any one of them is missing, the extension emits no telemetry.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Your organization enables telemetry through browser enterprise policy</b><span class="tx">That policy is distributed by the browser's management mechanism and cannot be changed by a repository. A repository cannot enable telemetry for you.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Your organization provides an <code>https:</code> collection endpoint</b><span class="tx">The endpoint belongs to your organization. There is no default collection endpoint, and the extension does not contain one pointing to Reefact or to the extension's authors.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>You explicitly enable telemetry on that device</b><span class="tx">The consent control shows the collection endpoint it applies to. Your consent applies only to that endpoint, is stored locally on the device, and <strong>is not synchronized</strong> through browser sync. Disabling telemetry takes effect immediately for open tabs, and remains possible even after the policy has stopped declaring an endpoint.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout acc">
          <span class="ct">if all three conditions are met</span>
          <h4>Only aggregated data is sent. Never free text.</h4>
          <p>The payload may contain the repository being viewed, the active configuration mode, and counters such as labels used — each with its kind, <code>remark</code>, <code>status</code> or <code>reply</code> —, diagnostic codes, and degraded selector rules. It contains <strong>no free-form text</strong>. If a value does not match the expected structure, the extension drops it rather than transmitting it. Telemetry requests include neither cookies nor a referrer, so the URL of the review page is not sent along with the request. This behaviour is verified in a real browser as part of the automated checks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">what the extension does not do:</span>
          <h2>Four explicit guarantees</h2>
        </div>
        <ul class="bul measure">
          <li>It sends <strong>no comment, code, or diff content</strong> to Reefact, the extension's authors, or any other third party.</li>
          <li>It executes <strong>no remote code</strong>. Its security policy prevents scripts from being loaded unless they are packaged with the extension, and no dependency is loaded from a CDN.</li>
          <li>It does not sell or share data for advertising purposes.</li>
          <li>It does not track your browsing outside review pages on supported code platforms.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small measure">The source code is public and auditable under the Apache-2.0 license. The repository documents why each browser permission is required, and holds the privacy policy that this page restates.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>You don't have to take our word for any of this.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">The behaviour described here can be verified directly in the source code, and the published extension bundle is checked on every release.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('howitworks', 'en')}>How it works</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
